# Copy to .env.local (gitignored) and adjust as needed.
# yahoo-finance2 needs no API key — these are optional tuning knobs.

# Chart history depth: Yahoo Finance's free fundamentalsTimeSeries API has a
# hard, undocumented backend cap of ~4 annual periods / ~5 quarters *no
# matter what date range is requested*. This app has no source deep enough
# to back a genuine 10-year+ range selection (there is no configuration that
# unlocks this — it's a hard limitation of the free data sources this app
# uses, not a documented optional integration). A "10 Years"/"All Available"
# range selection will only ever show however much Yahoo (+ FMP, if
# FMP_API_KEY is set below) actually has, usually ~4 years.
#
# How long quotes/search results are cached in-process before refetching (ms).
MARKET_DATA_CACHE_TTL_MS=20000

# Optional secondary data source (lib/finance/providers/fmp.ts). Backfills
# cash-flow fields (stock-based comp, capex) when Yahoo's free tier omits
# them for a given ticker. Get a free key at financialmodelingprep.com —
# leave unset and FinLens runs Yahoo-only with no loss of functionality.
FMP_API_KEY=

# --- Multi-User Authentication & Data Isolation (lib/db/client.ts) -------
#
# This app's user/session/portfolio database is Postgres, hosted on Neon.
# There is deliberately no "zero-config local file" fallback (unlike an
# earlier version of this app, which used Turso/SQLite): Vercel's
# serverless functions have a read-only, ephemeral filesystem, so a file on
# disk cannot correctly back multi-user data once deployed there — that's
# true no matter how the file is created, so rather than let it silently
# "work" on one machine and break in production, the app fails loudly with
# a clear error (see dbErrorJson in lib/http/noStore.ts) if this isn't set.
#
# Setup — no CLI, no separate account signup, nothing to copy/paste:
#   1. Open your project in the Vercel dashboard -> Storage tab
#   2. Click "Create Database" / "Add Integration" -> choose Neon
#   3. Follow the prompts (this creates a Neon account + Postgres database
#      for you automatically) and confirm
# That's it — Vercel automatically injects DATABASE_URL (and the legacy
# POSTGRES_URL alias) into this project's Production, Preview, AND
# Development environments. Nothing to paste into Environment Variables
# by hand.
#
# For local development, pull that same value down instead of retyping it:
#   npm i -g vercel   (one-time)
#   vercel link       (one-time, connects this folder to the Vercel project)
#   vercel env pull .env.development.local
# Or, if you'd rather skip the CLI entirely, open the Vercel dashboard's
# Environment Variables page and copy DATABASE_URL's value into your own
# .env.local by hand.
DATABASE_URL=

# --- Secure API Keys Migration (lib/security/encryption.ts) --------------
#
# Server-only secret used to encrypt (AES-256-GCM) each user's own
# Finnhub/Polygon/Alpha Vantage API keys before they're stored in Postgres
# (the api_keys table) — see lib/db/apiKeys.ts. Required for
# /api/settings/api-keys (Settings page -> API Keys & Data Sources) to
# work at all; like DATABASE_URL, there's no insecure fallback — a missing
# secret fails loudly rather than ever writing a plaintext key.
#
# Generate one (any long random string works; scrypt derives the actual
# AES key from it, so it doesn't need to be exactly 32 bytes itself):
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# Then set it in Vercel's Project Settings -> Environment Variables
# (Production, Preview, AND Development) and, for local dev, either pull
# it down with `vercel env pull .env.development.local` alongside
# DATABASE_URL, or paste the same value into your own .env.local.
#
# IMPORTANT: changing this value after keys are already saved makes every
# previously-saved key permanently undecryptable (decrypt() will throw) —
# generate it once and keep it stable, the same way you would any other
# production secret/signing key.
API_KEY_ENCRYPTION_SECRET=

# --- Natural Language Strategy Builder (lib/ai/anthropic.ts) -------------
#
# Server-only Anthropic API key, used to parse free-text screening
# strategies (English or Hebrew) into structured filters — see
# lib/strategy/parse.ts. This is FinLens's own app-level credential
# (billed to this app), completely separate from the per-user Finnhub/
# Polygon/Alpha Vantage keys above: there is no per-user LLM key, every
# user's strategy query goes through this one key. Required for the
# Strategy Builder page (/strategy) and its /api/strategy route to work;
# like DATABASE_URL and API_KEY_ENCRYPTION_SECRET, a missing key fails
# loudly (a clear 503 from the route) rather than silently degrading.
#
# Create a key at console.anthropic.com/settings/keys, then set it in
# Vercel's Project Settings -> Environment Variables (Production, Preview,
# AND Development) and, for local dev, either pull it down with
# `vercel env pull .env.development.local` alongside the vars above, or
# paste the same value into your own .env.local.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# --- Strategy Builder Universe Refresh Cron (app/api/cron/refresh-strategy-universe) ---
#
# Protects the background job that precomputes RSI/SMA/quote data for the
# Strategy Builder's screening universe (lib/strategy/universe-refresh.ts)
# into Postgres, so live screening queries read from that table instead of
# making live Yahoo Finance calls (and hitting Yahoo's rate limits) on
# every request. Vercel automatically sends `Authorization: Bearer
# <CRON_SECRET>` on its own cron-triggered requests once this is set as an
# env var on the project — see vercel.json for the schedule. Without this
# set, the route refuses to run at all (a public, unauthenticated endpoint
# that triggers ~180 live market-data fetches per hit would be a real
# abuse/cost vector) rather than silently allowing unauthenticated access.
#
# Generate one the same way as API_KEY_ENCRYPTION_SECRET above:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# Then set it in Vercel's Project Settings -> Environment Variables
# (Production, Preview, AND Development). Not needed for local dev unless
# you're manually testing the cron route with a curl request that sets the
# same Authorization header by hand.
CRON_SECRET=

# --- Password Reset Emails (Resend) ---------------------------------------
#
# Server-only API key used by lib/email/resend.ts to actually email a
# password-reset link/token to the user (POST /api/auth/request-password-reset)
# instead of only printing it to the server console. Like FMP_API_KEY, this
# is an optional integration with a documented, non-broken fallback: leaving
# it unset does NOT break password resets —
# isEmailConfigured() in lib/email/resend.ts detects the missing key and the
# route falls straight back to logging the token (visible via `vercel logs`
# in production or your terminal in local dev), exactly as it did before
# this was wired up.
#
# Get a free key at resend.com (no credit card required for the free tier):
#   1. Sign up at resend.com and create an API key (Dashboard -> API Keys)
#   2. Set it in Vercel's Project Settings -> Environment Variables
#      (Production, Preview, AND Development) and, for local dev, either
#      pull it down with `vercel env pull .env.development.local` alongside
#      the vars above, or paste the same value into your own .env.local.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# Optional — the "From" address reset emails are sent with. Resend's free
# tier can only send from onboarding@resend.dev until you verify your own
# domain in the Resend dashboard (Domains tab); lib/email/resend.ts already
# falls back to "Stox <onboarding@resend.dev>" if this is left unset, so you
# don't need to touch this until you've verified a real domain. Once you
# have, set this to something like:
#   RESEND_FROM_EMAIL="Stox <noreply@yourdomain.com>"
RESEND_FROM_EMAIL=
